# Copy to .env and fill in. Only the first two are required.
#
# Nothing here configures the CONNECTOR: it has no environment-variable layer,
# so every connector value — including every price this station charges — lives
# in ./connector.toml, and its keys are FILES you generate next to it
# (README step 2).
#
# Nothing here is a secret either. The stream key, the RTMPS private key and
# the connector's keys are all MOUNTED FILES, never environment values: an
# environment variable is visible to anything that can read /proc, shows up in
# `docker inspect`, and ends up in shell history on the way in.

# ── Required ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The public hostname of the connector's client edge — where a viber's paid
# pulls land. The only public HTTP name a station has; nothing free is served
# from a station node, so there is no second one.
EDGE_HOST=station.demo.example.com
# Where Let's Encrypt sends certificate-expiry notices.
ACME_EMAIL=you@example.com

# ── The ladder ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which rungs this station offers, and therefore which addresses a viber can
# buy. KEEP THIS IN STEP WITH ./connector.toml: a rung with no route there is
# unreachable and unsellable, and a route naming a rung that is not here is a
# paid 404.
#
# `<name>:<height>:<video bitrate>:<audio bitrate>` for a rung with a picture,
# `<name>:<audio bitrate>` for one carrying only sound. Bitrates are CAPS, not
# targets. The origin recomputes cap × duration at every start and refuses to
# start — naming the rung — if one could exceed ADR 0001's 2 MiB segment
# budget. At four-second segments the ceiling is 4.19 Mbit/s.
STATION_RUNGS=audio:128k,480p:480:800k:128k,720p:720:1800k:128k,1080p:1080:3000k:128k

# Segment duration, fixed. Fifteen segments a minute at the default, so a flat
# per-segment price is a per-minute rate divided by fifteen. Changing this
# changes what every price in connector.toml buys, and it changes the byte
# budget arithmetic — raise it and the ladder above may stop fitting.
STATION_SEGMENT_SECONDS=4

# How many segments each rung keeps. A sliding window evicted by count: older
# spans are unlinked as the broadcast runs, so a broadcast that lasts for days
# does not fill the disk. The bound is arithmetic you can do from these two
# lines — the window times the ladder's worst-case segment, ≈175 MiB on the
# defaults above — and the origin prints it at boot. A window of zero is
# refused at boot; the newest segment is never evicted.
STATION_RETAIN_SEGMENTS=60

# How long a publish may go without sending vibes before the station takes
# itself off the air. An uplink that dies quietly does not close its socket:
# the far end vanishes, no FIN and no RST arrive, and the connection sits
# half-open until TCP eventually gives up — which behind a NAT that has
# forgotten the flow is never. Without this bound `/now` would go on reporting
# `live: true` beside a sequence that never moves, and a viber could not tell
# that from a station whose edge has merely stalled.
#
# The clock counts VIBES, not socket activity, so a publisher that is connected
# and sending nothing is a stalled edge and the station says so. Going off the
# air changes nothing a viber can buy: the window already produced stays
# servable at its sequences, and a broadcaster who reconnects is accepted and
# continues the sequence rather than restarting it at zero. Whole seconds, at
# least 1; there is no value that switches the rule off, and one that would is
# refused at boot.
STATION_INGEST_IDLE_SECONDS=30

# ── Images ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The station origin app image only. It defaults to `:release`, which moves on
# every green merge to this repo's main and is what the Watchtower overlay
# follows. Pin an immutable `:sha-<short>` tag here to hold a specific build or
# roll one back.
#
# The CONNECTOR is deliberately NOT settable here: it is pinned to an immutable
# tag directly in ./docker-compose.yml, next to the connector.toml it was
# validated against, so the two move in one reviewed commit and reach the box
# in one `git pull`. Adopting a newer connector means editing that `image:`
# line, which is the only place in this repository a connector build may be
# named.
STATION_ORIGIN_IMAGE=ghcr.io/toon-protocol/station-origin:release
